    <item>
      <title>Do looped transformers write reasoning they don't use?</title>
      <link>https://igort99.github.io/posts/2026/10/05/looped-cot-faithfulness/</link>
      <description>TL;DR: Huginn uses its reasoning. More loops didn’t change that.
How I got this idea What is a looped transformer Experiment Results Where each model stops needing the trace A probe that looked like a result and was not What this does and does not show How I got this idea So I was browsing HackerNews the other day and read a blog about the gpt6-astra [1] that speculated Astra may be using looped transformer logic introduced by earlier research work [2]. I like reading these since new ideas always come to my mind. This topic was somewhat familiar to me, but I was curious what is all the fuss about looped transformers hiding their reasoning traces and that they may be inferring most from their hidden states. Naturally I would search the web to try to find some research that already checked this out, but I couldn’t find any that tested exactly this (would like to see if I missed some somehow; there is just too much research going on at any given moment). The closest I found is Lu et al. [3], who looked inside Huginn with the reasoning turned off and found little sign of it reasoning in its loop. But I couldn’t find anyone who checked whether a looped model actually uses the reasoning it writes. So this post is about how I tried to verify and check this, but first let’s shortly jump in to see what the hell a looped transformer is.</description>
      <category>ai</category>
      <guid>https://igort99.github.io/posts/2026/10/05/looped-cot-faithfulness/</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 03:02:36 GMT</pubDate>
    </item>
